WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.660
People in the United States still want
to be part of the American Dream story

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.480
of success. But what actually is the
American Dream, and is it still

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.049
attainable? Traditionally, the dream has
been associated with a successful career,

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:20.550
wealth, and homeownership. Popular
conceptions of the American Dream also

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.689
focused on the ability to achieve more
success than one's parents, causing some

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:28.980
to believe that the opportunity to
realize the dream is declining. Yet the

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.059
results of the AEI Survey on Community
and Society suggests something different.

00:00:33.059 --> 00:00:36.600
Significantly more people believe that
the freedom to live their lives as they

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.290
choose, and the ability to have
fulfilling family lives are more

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.920
essential to the dream than
homeownership, wealth, and having a better

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.370
quality of life than their parents. We
found that about 8 in 10 Americans

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.170
believe that they are living the dream
or are on their way to achieving it, and

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.739
only 16% of people say that becoming
wealthy is essential to the American

00:00:55.739 --> 00:00:59.460
Dream.This is good news for Americans
today. Our results show that people still

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.699
believe that family and community matter,
and that the majority of Americans

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.140
believe that the American Dream is in
their reach.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.040
Is the American Dream still

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.420
alive for you? Let us know in our poll.
Also, let us know what other topics you'd

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.320
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,
and be sure to like and subscribe for

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.150
more research and videos from AEI.

